<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.13.3: Finding Domain and Range Using a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01096</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.13.3: Finding Domain and Range Using a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b943d03e-b169-4373-ac2c-61f1fc49d1ce</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A tennis ball was dropped from a certain height. It bounced several times, rolled along for a short period, and then stopped. Function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>H</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> gives its height over time.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Here is a partial graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>H</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Height is measured in feet. Time is measured in seconds.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">Use the graph to help you answer the questions.</para>
      <para id="para-00004">Be prepared to explain what each value or set of values means in this situation.</para>
      <para id="para-00005">
          <media alt="Nonlinear function. Time (seconds) and height (feet).">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Find <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>H</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. What point is this on the graph? What does it represent?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>H</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. It tells us the ball was dropped from a height of 6 feet at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Solve <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>H</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.54</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1.48</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.25</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> are three possible solutions, but there are others. Each one represents the time when the ball hit the ground.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Describe the domain of the function.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">The domain includes all values between 0 and the time when the ball stopped moving, which, based on the graph, is about 3.8.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Describe the range of the function.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">The range includes all values between 0 and 6 feet, the height from which the ball was dropped.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End --></section>
      <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Interpreting Function Notation</title>
      <para id="para-00018">Watch the following video to learn more about function notation.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/20f903eb50ca52bd9b9bfa4a7db8be6af298e6be"/>
      </media></section>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
          <section id="sect-00004">
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00019">In function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>H</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the input was time in seconds, and the output was height in feet.</para>
          <para id="para-00020">Think about some other quantities that could be inputs or outputs in this situation.</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Describe a function whose domain includes only integers. Be sure to specify the units.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00023">The maximum height, in feet, that the ball reaches after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> bounces as a function of the number of bounces.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00024">Sketch a graph of this function. Write down your answer then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your answer.  </para>
        <para id="para-00025"><media alt="A blank graph. Origin O. "><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_02.svg"/></media></para>
  
            <para id="para-00026">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your sketch and answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00028">
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_03.svg"/>
                </media>
              
              <emphasis id="docs-internal-guid-663b6ac4-7fff-f1cb-6e91-14df360308a7"/>
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00029">Describe a function whose range includes only integers. Be sure to specify the units.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00030">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00031">The number of times the ball has hit the ground as a function of time, in seconds, since the ball was dropped.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00032">Sketch a graph of this function. Write down your answer then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your answer. 
        <media alt="A blank graph. Origin O. "><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_04.svg"/></media></para>
            <para id="para-00033">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your sketch and answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00035">
                      <media alt="A blank graph. Origin O. ">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_05.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00036">The graph below shows the number of customers, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in a restaurant <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> hours after 8 a.m.</para>
      <para id="para-00037">What does <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> equal, and what does it mean?</para>
      <para id="para-00038">
          <media alt="A GRAPHED FUNCTION, WHERE NUMBER OF CUSTOMERS IS A FUNCTION OF THE NUMBER OF HOURS AFTER 8 AM. THE GRAPH INCREASES, DECREASES, INCREASES, AND THEN DECREASES AGAIN.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_07_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section id="sect-00006">
      <title>Domain and Range from Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00039">The graph below shows the distance Carl drives from home, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>D</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is time in seconds.</para>
      <para id="para-00040">
          <media alt="Graph on coordinate grid, origin O.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_08_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>What is the domain of the graph?</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00041">The domain is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values that are at least 0 and a maximum of 400.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item>What is the range of the graph?</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00042">The range is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values that are at least 0 and a maximum of 285.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
        <item>What are the values of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>D</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and what do they mean?</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00043"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>D</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>D</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>400</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and this is when Carl left home and then arrived home, so the distance was 0.</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
  <title>Try It: Domain and Range from Graphs</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00044">What are the values for the domain and range of the graph below?</para>
            <para id="para-00045">
                      <media alt="A graph. ">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_08_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00046">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00047">Here is how to find the domain and range of the graph:</para>
            <para id="para-00048">The domain includes the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values or inputs of the graph. The domain is from at least 0 to at most 10.</para>
            <para id="para-00049">The range includes the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values or outputs of the graph. The range is from at least 1 to at most 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    
  </content>
</document>
